21:["$","$L26",null,{"items":[{"question":"What is the standard calculation method used across India?","answer":"Most masajid, Darul Ulooms, and Islamic councils across India (including Deoband, Nadwa, and Sunni regional boards) utilize the University of Islamic Sciences Karachi method with an 18-degree Fajr twilight angle and 18-degree Isha twilight angle."},{"question":"Why does Bangalore prayer time differ from Delhi by up to 45 minutes?","answer":"India spans nearly 30 degrees of longitude and 29 degrees of latitude. Bangalore lies in southern India at latitude 12.97°N and longitude 77.59°E, whereas Delhi is located at latitude 28.61°N and longitude 77.20°E. The northern latitude of Delhi causes dramatic seasonal daylight shifts, whereas equatorial Bangalore maintains more consistent day-night lengths throughout the year."},{"question":"What school of juristic thought is followed for Asr in Calicut and Kerala?","answer":"The Muslim community in Kerala and Coastal Karnataka predominantly follows the Shafi’i madhhab. Therefore, masajid in Calicut, Kochi, and Malappuram generally call the Asr Azan at the earlier (Factor 1) shadow timing."},{"question":"How does India’s single timezone affect prayer times between the East and West?","answer":"Because India uses a single time zone (IST / UTC+5:30 based on 82.5°E near Mirzapur), cities in eastern India (like Kolkata or Guwahati) experience sunrise and sunset more than an hour earlier on the clock than cities in western India (like Mumbai, Ahmedabad, or Surat)."}],"title":"Key Questions & Rulings Answered"}]
